# 🔥🔥🔥 Patterns You Should Memorize

### Largest

```js
let largest = numbers[0];

for (let num of numbers) {
  if (num > largest) {
    largest = num;
  }
}
```

### Smallest

```js
let smallest = numbers[0];

for (let num of numbers) {
  if (num < smallest) {
    smallest = num;
  }
}
```

### Frequency

```js
const frequency = {};

for (let num of numbers) {
  frequency[num] = (frequency[num] || 0) + 1;
}
```

### Remove duplicates

```js
const result = [...new Set(numbers)];
```

### Find duplicates

```js
const seen = new Set();
const duplicates = new Set();

for (let num of numbers) {
  if (seen.has(num)) {
    duplicates.add(num);
  } else {
    seen.add(num);
  }
}
```

### 🧠 One-Line Memory Trick

```text
Largest          → biggest
Smallest         → smallest
Second-largest   → keep top 2
Remove duplicates → Set
Find duplicates  → seen + Set
Frequency        → value → count
```

The **biggest pattern to understand here** is that `frequency` and `Set` solve a huge number of interview problems:

```text
frequency
→ "How many times?"

Set
→ "Have I seen this before?"
```

Once these two ideas become automatic, many array questions that initially look tricky become much easier.

