# 6. 🔥🔥 Group objects by a property

This is another **very important interview pattern**.

Suppose:

```js
const users = [
  { name: "Ajay", department: "IT" },
  { name: "Rahul", department: "HR" },
  { name: "Amit", department: "IT" },
  { name: "Priya", department: "HR" },
];
```

We want to group them by `department`.

Expected:

```text
{
  IT: [
    { name: "Ajay", department: "IT" },
    { name: "Amit", department: "IT" }
  ],

  HR: [
    { name: "Rahul", department: "HR" },
    { name: "Priya", department: "HR" }
  ]
}
```

---

# 🔥🔥 Method 1 — Using `reduce()`

```js
function groupBy(users, property) {
  return users.reduce((groups, user) => {
    const key = user[property];

    if (!groups[key]) {
      groups[key] = [];
    }

    groups[key].push(user);

    return groups;
  }, {});
}

const users = [
  { name: "Ajay", department: "IT" },
  { name: "Rahul", department: "HR" },
  { name: "Amit", department: "IT" },
  { name: "Priya", department: "HR" },
];

console.log(groupBy(users, "department"));
```

---

# 🧠 This looks difficult, so let's slow down

The important idea is:

```text
property value
      ↓
becomes a key
      ↓
objects with same key go into same array
```

For example:

```text
Ajay → IT
Amit → IT
```

Both go into:

```text
IT: []
```

Similarly:

```text
Rahul → HR
Priya → HR
```

go into:

```text
HR: []
```

---

# 🔥🔥 Step-by-step

Initially:

```text
groups = {}
```

First object:

```text
{
  name: "Ajay",
  department: "IT"
}
```

Get:

```js
const key = user[property];
```

Here:

```text
property = "department"
```

So:

```text
key = "IT"
```

Does:

```text
groups.IT
```

exist?

No.

So:

```js
groups[key] = [];
```

Now:

```text
groups = {
  IT: []
}
```

Then:

```js
groups[key].push(user);
```

Now:

```text
groups = {
  IT: [
    { name: "Ajay", department: "IT" }
  ]
}
```

---

# 🔥 Next object

Rahul:

```text
department = "HR"
```

Create:

```text
HR: []
```

Then add Rahul.

Now:

```text
{
  IT: [
    Ajay
  ],

  HR: [
    Rahul
  ]
}
```

---

# 🔥 Next object

Amit:

```text
department = "IT"
```

`IT` already exists.

So:

```js
groups.IT.push(Amit);
```

Now:

```text
IT: [
  Ajay,
  Amit
]
```

---

# 🔥 Final object

```text
{
  IT: [
    Ajay,
    Amit
  ],

  HR: [
    Rahul,
    Priya
  ]
}
```

---

# 🔥🔥🔥 Why use `reduce()`?

Because we are converting:

```text
Array
```

into:

```text
Object
```

And `reduce()` is perfect when we want to build one final result.

Think:

```text
reduce()
→ "I want to process everything and build ONE result."
```

---

# 🔥🔥 Group by another property

The same function can group by:

```text
department
age
city
role
category
status
```

Example:

```js
const products = [
  { name: "Phone", category: "Electronics" },
  { name: "Shirt", category: "Clothing" },
  { name: "Laptop", category: "Electronics" },
  { name: "Jeans", category: "Clothing" },
];

console.log(groupBy(products, "category"));
```

Result:

```text
{
  Electronics: [
    Phone,
    Laptop
  ],

  Clothing: [
    Shirt,
    Jeans
  ]
}
```

---

# 🔥🔥🔥 Group by property — simpler fixed version

If the interviewer specifically asks:

> Group users by department.

You don't need to make it generic.

```js
function groupByDepartment(users) {
  return users.reduce((groups, user) => {
    const department = user.department;

    if (!groups[department]) {
      groups[department] = [];
    }

    groups[department].push(user);

    return groups;
  }, {});
}
```

This is easier to explain in an interview.

---

# 🔥🔥🔥 Important Pattern

This pattern:

```js
if (!groups[key]) {
  groups[key] = [];
}

groups[key].push(item);
```

is extremely important.

It means:

```text
Does this group exist?

No
↓
Create it

Yes
↓
Add to existing group
```

Memorize this pattern.

---

# 🔥🔥🔥 Predict the output

```js
const users = [
  { name: "A", role: "developer" },
  { name: "B", role: "designer" },
  { name: "C", role: "developer" },
